<section class="case-sec" id="premise">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§A — The question nobody could answer</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        "Can I rely on this data right now?" Before this platform, answering that
        meant finding the person who built the pipeline, and hoping they still
        worked here. The org had plenty of dashboards and no way to establish
        whether the tables underneath them were fresh, complete, or shaped the
        way the consumer assumed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The failure that motivated it is documented in its sibling case file: an
        upstream change that <a href="/case/revenue-attribution/">silently broke
        revenue attribution</a> while the pipeline kept happily producing rows.
        A system that can't detect its own wrongness will eventually be wrong
        expensively, and the interval before it is discovered is a function of
        luck.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="metric-row panel reveal">
    <div class="m"><span class="v">400+</span><span class="d">production datasets profiled hourly</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">~88%</span><span class="d">silent row loss caught before it reached financial reports</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">org-wide</span><span class="d">discovery &amp; governance portal launch</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">4</span><span class="d">core backing services designed</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="checks">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§B — Checks that can fail a run, not a report that nobody opens</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The platform is self-service by design: a team registers a dataset and
        gets automated <strong>freshness</strong>, <strong>volume</strong>, and
        <strong>schema</strong> anomaly detection without writing monitoring code. The
        distinction that makes it useful is that these are
        <strong>quality gates</strong>, not notifications — a failing check can stop
        the run rather than annotate the wreckage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Underneath, <strong>hourly table-health profiling</strong> runs across
        <strong>400+ production datasets</strong>, which is what makes anomaly
        detection possible at all: you can't call a row count anomalous without a
        distribution to compare it to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="table-panel panel reveal">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>// check families</span><span class="g">what each one catches that the others miss</span></div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Question it asks</th><th>Failure it catches</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">Freshness</td><td>did this land when it should have?</td><td class="mono dim">upstream stalled; consumers reading stale truth</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">Volume</td><td>is the row count in its own distribution?</td><td class="mono dim">silent partial loads — the 88% case</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">Schema</td><td>did the shape change underneath consumers?</td><td class="mono dim">columns dropped, retyped, or quietly renamed</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="board-foot">// volume is the boring one and it is the one that paid for the platform. <span class="g">a pipeline that returns 12% of its rows returns them successfully.</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="catch">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§C — The first named catch</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        An upstream change collapsed roughly <strong>88% of rows</strong> in a critical
        source. Nothing errored. The job succeeded, the tables populated, the
        refresh completed — and the numbers were heading for
        <strong>financial reports</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The volume check fired on the distribution, not on a threshold somebody
        had guessed at, and the gate held the data before a single dashboard —
        or Finance — saw a wrong number. That's the catch the platform is named
        by internally, and it's the reason adoption stopped needing to be sold.
      </p>
      <p class="mono-note">
        // the argument for this kind of platform is never made in the abstract.
        <span class="g">it's made once, by a catch nobody can argue with.</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="portal">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§D — From a checker to a portal: discovery, ownership, lineage</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Checks tell you a dataset is broken. They don't tell you what it is, who
        owns it, what feeds it, or who breaks if you change it. So the quality
        platform grew into an internal <strong>data discovery and governance
        portal</strong>, launched <strong>org-wide</strong>: dataset search, ownership,
        lineage visualization, quality signals, and usage insight in one surface,
        so any engineer can answer "can I rely on this data right now?" without
        finding a person.
      </p>
      <p>
        I designed its <strong>four core backing services</strong>:
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
