<section class="case-sec" id="lineage">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§E — The lineage engine: an LLM that reads pipeline code</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Lineage is normally harvested from an orchestrator's declared
        dependencies — which is fine until the real logic lives inside the job
        rather than in its configuration. In a 20+ component legacy estate, the
        declared graph and the actual data flow are different objects, and the
        difference is exactly where incidents hide.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the lineage engine <strong>reads the pipeline code with an LLM</strong> and
        maps the production data flow from what the code actually does — the
        joins, the writes, the derived tables — rather than from what the
        scheduler was told. This is the piece I'd most want to talk through in an
        interview: it's the clearest case I have of using a language model for a
        job that classical tooling had structurally failed at, rather than
        bolting a chat box onto something that already worked.
      </p>
      <p class="mono-note">
        // the honest caveat: code-derived lineage is <span class="g">high recall, imperfect precision.</span>
        it earns trust as a map of where to look, and it is validated against
        observed access patterns rather than believed on sight.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="throttling">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§F — The same discipline, pointed at customers: 99% of throttling incidents</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Data trust and customer trust are the same engineering problem viewed
        from either end. Storage customers hitting performance throttling
        experienced it as the platform failing them; internally it arrived as a
        stream of individually-handled incidents with no fleet view.
      </p>
      <p>
        Building <strong>fleet-wide telemetry pipelines</strong> and an
        <strong>early-warning dashboard with automated triage</strong> turned that from
        reactive incident handling into detection before impact —
        <strong>eliminating 99% of performance-throttling incidents for the
        platform's most critical customers</strong>, and <strong>92% across all paying
        customers</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gap between those two numbers is the interesting part, and I'll
        defend it: the most critical customers got the deeper instrumentation
        first because that's where a single incident is worth millions a month.
        The 92% is what generalized once the fleet pipeline existed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="metric-row panel reveal">
    <div class="m"><span class="v">99%</span><span class="d">throttling incidents eliminated, most critical customers</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">92%</span><span class="d">across all paying customers</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">10+</span><span class="d">at-risk accounts retained, each worth millions per month</span></div>
    <div class="m"><span class="v">16%</span><span class="d">faster mitigation across 8+ engineering teams</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="governance">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§G — Governance is the unglamorous half</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A trustworthy data estate also has to be a defensible one. I led
        <strong>security and compliance modernization</strong> for the team's data
        estate: a <strong>service-authentication overhaul</strong>, a
        <strong>GDPR-driven data cleanup</strong> run with the security org, and
        <strong>15+ pipeline migrations</strong> off a deprecated platform.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of that produces a number anyone celebrates. It's on this page
        because a candidate who only shows the interesting work is showing you an
        incomplete engineer — and because "we can't move that pipeline, nobody
        knows what depends on it" is precisely the problem the lineage engine
        above was built to delete.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
